  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1) \nMid level ridging centered south of the area will continue hot\ntemperatures across the region today and tomorrow. Highs may end up\nbeing just a shade higher than yesterday. But it also appears that\ndew points may mix out and be a bit lower. All in all, heat indices\nshould end up within a few degrees of 100. There remains the\npotential for the heat index to be in that same range again on Friday\nacross the southern half of the forecast area. And if that occurs,\nthen the heat advisory would need to be extended. But there is not\nenough confidence at this time to do so. Signals of MCS remnants\nmoving across the area on Friday create a large amount of uncertainty\nin the temperature forecast for then.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2)\nShort wave coming over the top of the ridge and then diving east\nsoutheast Thursday afternoon and evening will develop thunderstorms.\nWould expect much of this activity to be north and east of the\nforecast area. But there is certainly potential for central Ohio to\nbe affected with much lower chances/confidence further south and\nwest. Bulk shear will be pretty marginal during the day, but improve\nsomewhat across the northeast part of the forecast area in the\nevening as the short wave passes by. While not at its peak, there \nwill still be plenty of instability lingering into the evening. All \ntogether, there will be the potential for strong to severe storms \nlate Thursday into Thursday evening, particularly for central Ohio.\n\nA mid level low pressure system will deepen and dig across the \nMaritimes and northern New England late in the week which will cause\nthe high to retrograde back into the center of the country. This \nwill put our region into northwest flow aloft, which will make us\nprone to MCSs. Much of the guidance suite is suggesting that MCS\nremnants may move across the area on Friday morning and then possibly\nagain Saturday morning. At this point, it appears that there would\nnot be much instability to work with at the times they are currently\nforecast to move through, but cannot completely rule out concern for\nsome strong to severe storms. Precipitable water will increase to \n1.6 to 1.8 inches and there will be a deep warm cloud layer, so \nlocally heavy rain may come into play. It is very unclear, whether\nany part of the area will recover enough to have additional\nconvection in the afternoon and evening which theoretically would\nhave more instability to work with.\n\nWe know that MCSs can make substantial changes to the energy budget\nof the atmosphere which then alters where subsequent convection may \ndevelop. So the uncertainty in this forecast cycle increases quite a\nbit over time.",
